C = [
("h1", "1. The Earlier Voices"),
("p", "Claim: before any writing prophet speaks against the sacrifices, a prophet and a king speak from inside the Law, and what they say sets the pattern the later prophets follow. Samuel sets obedience above sacrifice and then goes back with the king to the place of worship. David says the LORD did not desire sacrifice and offering, and then says what he delights to do. Asaph hears the LORD say he will not reprove Israel for its sacrifices, and then hears him reprove the worshipers. In none of the passages is the order abolished."),
("sub", "1.1 Saul at Gilgal"),
("p", "The earliest sentence in the series is spoken to a king who has kept part of a command. The LORD had sent Saul to “utterly destroy the sinners the Amalekites” (1 Samuel 15:18). Saul comes back with the best of the sheep and oxen, and Samuel hears them: “What meaneth then this bleating of the sheep in mine ears, and the lowing of the oxen which I hear?” (1 Samuel 15:14). Saul says the people spared the best “to sacrifice unto the LORD thy God” (1 Samuel 15:15), and again, of the chief of the spoil, “to sacrifice unto the LORD thy God in Gilgal” (1 Samuel 15:21)."),
("p", "The offering Saul describes is real. It is a sacrifice to the LORD, at a place of sacrifice, from the best of the flock. The text does not say the offering would have been wrongly made if it had been made in obedience. It says the animals were taken against a command, and the pretext for the taking was the offering. Saul’s sentence is the first form of a mistake the prophets will name again and again: that the offering can be brought as a payment to cover what the worshiper has done."),
("sub", "1.2 To obey is better than sacrifice"),
("p", "Samuel answers with a question and a statement. “Hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams” (1 Samuel 15:22). The grammar of the sentence is a comparison. Burnt offerings and sacrifices are named as things in which the LORD has delight. Obedience is set above them. The verse does not say that the LORD has no delight in sacrifice. It says he has more delight in the voice being obeyed."),
("p", "The next verse names what Saul has done in the terms of the Law’s gravest sins. “For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft, and stubbornness is as iniquity and idolatry. Because thou hast rejected the word of the LORD, he hath also rejected thee from being king” (1 Samuel 15:23). The charge is the rejection of the word of the LORD. The sacrifice is not what is rejected. Saul himself confesses in the following verse, “I have sinned: for I have transgressed the commandment of the LORD, and thy words” (1 Samuel 15:24), and he asks Samuel to “turn again with me, that I may worship the LORD” (1 Samuel 15:25)."),
("p", "Samuel first refuses, then goes. “I will not return with thee: for thou hast rejected the word of the LORD, and the LORD hath rejected thee from being king over Israel” (1 Samuel 15:26). But the chapter says a few verses later that “Samuel turned again after Saul; and Saul worshipped the LORD” (1 Samuel 15:31). The prophet who has just said obedience is better than sacrifice goes back with the king to the place of worship. He does not take the king’s worship to be void. He takes the king’s rule to be forfeited. The sentence of 15:22 is therefore a statement of rank inside a worship the prophet shares, and the pattern is already set: the offering stands, the man who brings it in disobedience is judged."),
("sub", "1.3 David: sacrifice and offering thou didst not desire"),
("p", "The Psalms add two voices from within the same order. In the fortieth psalm David says, “Sacrifice and offering thou didst not desire; mine ears hast thou opened: burnt offering and sin offering hast thou not required” (Psalm 40:6). The sentence names four kinds of offering, sacrifice, offering, burnt offering and sin offering, and says the LORD did not desire or require them. The next two verses give what he did desire. “Then said I, Lo, I come: in the volume of the book it is written of me, I delight to do thy will, O my God: yea, thy law is within my heart” (Psalm 40:7, 8)."),
("p", "The psalm sets the offerings beside the law. What is placed within the heart is the law, and the delight is in doing the will of God. The singer does not repudiate the book. He says it is written of him, and the law is within his heart. The sentence of verse 6 is the “not … but” that the volume will meet again at Hosea 6:6 and Jeremiah 7:22: not these offerings as the thing the LORD wants, but the will done from the heart. The psalm goes on, in verses 9 and 10, to the public declaration of what the singer has done: “I have preached righteousness in the great congregation: lo, I have not refrained my lips, O LORD, thou knowest” (Psalm 40:9). The great congregation is Israel assembled for worship, and the psalmist speaks there."),
("p", "The wording of verse 6, “mine ears hast thou opened,” is the sentence Hebrews 10 will quote in a different form. Chapter 8 reads that quotation. For now it is enough to say that Psalm 40:6 in the King James Version says what it says, and that the volume builds on no difference of wording."),
("sub", "1.4 Asaph: I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices"),
("p", "The fiftieth psalm is the clearest of the earlier voices on what the LORD leaves standing. God speaks as judge (Psalm 50:6), and he begins with his people: “Gather my saints together unto me; those that have made a covenant with me by sacrifice” (Psalm 50:5). The people are defined by a covenant made by sacrifice, and the LORD says so before he reproves them. Then comes the address: “Hear, O my people, and I will speak; O Israel, and I will testify against thee: I am God, even thy God” (Psalm 50:7)."),
("p", "The first thing the LORD says is what he does not charge. “I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices or thy burnt offerings, to have been continually before me” (Psalm 50:8). The sentence is an explicit statement that the sacrifices and burnt offerings, continually before him, are not the ground of the reproof. What follows is a rebuke of a mistaken idea of what the sacrifices are. “I will take no bullock out of thy house, nor he goats out of thy folds. For every beast of the forest is mine, and the cattle upon a thousand hills” (Psalm 50:9, 10). “If I were hungry, I would not tell thee: for the world is mine, and the fulness thereof. Will I eat the flesh of bulls, or drink the blood of goats?” (Psalm 50:12, 13)."),
("p", "The LORD does not say he has given no sacrifices. He says he does not need them as a man needs food, and that the people must not think of the offering as something they supply to him. In its place he names thank-offering and vow: “Offer unto God thanksgiving; and pay thy vows unto the most High: and call upon me in the day of trouble: I will deliver thee, and thou shalt glorify me” (Psalm 50:14, 15). Thanksgiving and vows are the language of the sacrifices of peace and of vows in Israel’s worship, and the LORD asks for them. The closing verse of the psalm says the same: “Whoso offereth praise glorifieth me: and to him that ordereth his conversation aright will I shew the salvation of God” (Psalm 50:23)."),
("p", "The second half of the psalm turns to the worshiper who recites the covenant and lives otherwise. “But unto the wicked God saith, What hast thou to do to declare my statutes, or that thou shouldest take my covenant in thy mouth? Seeing thou hatest instruction, and castest my words behind thee” (Psalm 50:16, 17). The list is of conduct, theft, adultery, deceit and slander (Psalm 50:18–20), and the LORD adds, “These things hast thou done, and I kept silence; thou thoughtest that I was altogether such an one as thyself: but I will reprove thee, and set them in order before thine eyes” (Psalm 50:21). The charge is a misjudgment of the LORD, that he is such a one as the worshiper, and it rests on the worshiper’s life, not on the altar."),
("sub", "1.5 David again: a broken spirit, then the walls, then the offerings"),
("p", "The fifty-first psalm is David’s confession after his sin, and it contains the sentence most often quoted against the altar. “For thou desirest not sacrifice; else would I give it: thou delightest not in burnt offering” (Psalm 51:16). It follows a prayer for cleansing, “Wash me throughly from mine iniquity, and cleanse me from my sin” (Psalm 51:2), and an acknowledgment that the sin is “against thee, thee only” (Psalm 51:4). The singer says that if sacrifice would answer, he would give it. He comes with no offering. What he brings is stated in the next verse. “The sacrifices of God are a broken spirit: a broken and a contrite heart, O God, thou wilt not despise” (Psalm 51:17)."),
("p", "Verses 18 and 19 are the sentences that decide how verse 16 is read. “Do good in thy good pleasure unto Zion: build thou the walls of Jerusalem. Then shalt thou be pleased with the sacrifices of righteousness, with burnt offering and whole burnt offering: then shall they offer bullocks upon thine altar” (Psalm 51:18, 19). The psalm that said the LORD does not delight in burnt offering ends by saying that when Zion is blessed and Jerusalem’s walls are built, the LORD will be pleased with burnt offering and whole burnt offering, and bullocks will be offered on his altar. The same singer, in the same psalm, says both. The only reading that keeps both is the reading of rank and of order: the offering is not what covers a sin of this kind, and it is received with pleasure when it is offered by those who have come with a broken heart and in a restored city."),
("p", "The volume reports verses 18 and 19 as written. It does not say when the walls are built or which altar is meant, and it builds nothing on the time. It says what the psalm says: the singer expects burnt offering and bullocks upon the altar to please the LORD."),
("ctbl", "The earlier voices in four passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What is said against the offering", "What is left standing"),
    ("1 Samuel 15:22", "Obedience is better than sacrifice; the fat of rams is not what the LORD delights in above his voice", "Burnt offerings and sacrifices are named as things in which the LORD has delight; Samuel returns with Saul and Saul worships (15:31)"),
    ("Psalm 40:6", "Sacrifice, offering, burnt offering and sin offering are not what the LORD desired or required", "The book and the law, and the delight to do his will, stand; the singer declares righteousness in the great congregation (40:7–10)"),
    ("Psalm 50:8–15", "The LORD does not need the beasts as food; the offering is not a supply to him", "“I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices”; thanksgiving and vows are asked; the charge falls on the wicked worshiper (50:16–21)"),
    ("Psalm 51:16–19", "The LORD does not delight in burnt offering for this sin; a broken spirit is the sacrifice", "When Zion is blessed and the walls built, the LORD is pleased with burnt offering and bullocks on his altar (51:18, 19)"),
  ], [0.2, 0.4, 0.4]),
("sub", "1.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Samuel, David and Asaph speak from inside the Law and the worship of Israel; none of them stands outside it to condemn it.",
    "Each sentence against sacrifice is a sentence of rank or of misuse: obedience above the fat of rams, the will done from the heart above the four offerings, the thank-offering and the vow above the supposed food of the altar, a broken spirit above a sacrifice that cannot cover this sin.",
    "Psalm 50:8 states in so many words what the LORD does not charge: “I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices.”",
    "Psalm 51:18, 19 and 1 Samuel 15:31 show the offerings and the worship still standing in the same passages.",
    "The pattern is set before the writing prophets: the sentence falls on the one who brings, in disobedience or in the thought that the LORD is such an one as himself.",
  ]),
]
